High-level design

Parse headers only and stop when you see CRLFCRLF.
Record:
header_end = index after "\r\n\r\n"
content_length (from headers)
initial_body_bytes = buffer.size() - header_end (may be 0)
Switch from buffering-in-memory to streaming-to-disk:
Create a temp file (use mkstemp) and write the initial_body_bytes to it.
Track bytes_written (initial_body_bytes).
Clear consumed data from in-memory buffer (keep any bytes after body if pipelining).
Keep the connection registered for EPOLLIN to receive more chunks.
On each subsequent recv:
Write newly received bytes directly to the temp file, increment bytes_written.
When bytes_written == content_length → upload complete: close temp file, move to final filename (atomic rename), queue response (201 or whatever) and switch to EPOLLOUT.
Error/timeout cases:
If bytes_written > content_length → respond 413 and delete temp file.
On I/O error → respond 500, delete temp file.
On timeout → you may delete partial file or keep for resume depending on policy.
Advantages:
Minimal memory usage (only small buffer and a temp file).
Works for arbitrarily large uploads (images, video, pdf, audio).
