Notes and best-practices

Use mkstemp to safely create temp files, set restrictive permissions.
Use atomic rename (rename()) to move temp file into final location.
Always check return values for write() and handle EINTR/EAGAIN properly (for non-blocking writes you might need to buffer leftover bytes — though for file descriptor writes to disk they're usually blocking; you can open temp file as blocking).
For chunked transfer-encoding you must implement chunk parsing; if you only support Content-Length, reject Transfer-Encoding header (501).
For multipart/form-data you may need to parse boundaries; for simple file uploads it's okay to save the raw body and let a downstream processor parse multipart parts.
Cleanup on connection close: delete temp file if partial and policy says so.
Initialize req.bytes_written and req.file_fd for each request.
Simpler alternative (if you prefer minimal change)

Keep existing buffer until body complete (works but memory-heavy for large files).
Or write only when body size exceeds threshold (e.g., 1 MB) — hybrid approach.
